It doesn't have to be "manipulated", or dug out by
the user just to make a bones file.

For example, if the PC is treated peacefully by
dwarves, and they keep getting randomly generated as
time goes by, they'll happily remove every wall on a
level except the self-healing ones, sooner or later.

Similarly if the PC stands on Elbereth or a Scare
Monster scroll for a very long time, the fleeing
digging type monsters will remove the walls.

This works especially well if the PC goes all around
the level carving new Elbereth messages and then
stalling atop them for a long while only killing
non-digging monsters for food or sacrificing, since
the hostile monsters seem to "orbit" the PC when
frightened.

Rock moles are your friends.

I've done that, with malice aforethought, to make a
level almost completely clear, while altar camping,
because I wanted to see the baddies coming well in
advance.

I got my PC killed by the usual YASD before the job
was entirely complete, though.

I set up a "blind" with boulders, one row away from
a far wall, so that monsters would be preferentially
generated on these few [5, iirc] squares of the
level out of my line of sight, and would first be
seen far away and not directly in line for ranged
attacks compared to me standing on the altar.

I did't have intrinsic telepathy, so this was
"second best".

As a result, I had most of a leveL "leveled" without
having to do a bit of digging myself.

Since all that was intended to be to my PC's real
benefit rather than just to make a perverse bones
file, I don't thing "manipulated" would have been an
appropriate term.

